WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.960
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:15.660
GRAY: I am pleased to
welcome to the platform

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:20.890
the honorable Alice K. Wolf,
mayor of the city of Cambridge,

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:24.540
and the honorable
Virgilio Barco, president

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:26.340
of the Republic of Colombia.

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:29.460
President Barco will
now give the address.

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:30.784
President Barco.

00:00:30.784 --> 00:00:32.772
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:58.616 --> 00:01:04.010
BARCO: President Gray,
members of the board,

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:08.950
distinguished guests,
class of 1990--

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:13.270
in a few short days,
each of you will be

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:16.420
entering a new stage in life--

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:21.460
new careers, new
opportunities, new challenges.

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:25.300
In a few short months, I
will be leaving the office

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:27.940
of President of Colombia.

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:32.170
I have been on a long
and fascinating journey

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:38.215
since that day in 1943
when, like all of you today,

00:01:38.215 --> 00:01:42.362
I received my degree from MIT.

00:01:42.362 --> 00:01:45.700
I remember that day well.

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:49.960
So many things raced
through my mind.

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:52.960
So much was happening
in the world.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:56.380
So much was happening so fast.

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:59.980
Social, political,
and economic orders

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:04.810
were turned upside down by
events around the world.

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:09.729
Overnight, it seemed borders
were changing in Europe.

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:14.460
No one was sure what
tomorrow will bring.

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:20.500
All of this must sound strangely
familiar to each of you

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:25.840
with the rapid rate of change
in Europe and around the world.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:30.070
All of it may be a bit
overwhelming as well.

00:02:30.070 --> 00:02:32.620
I can understand the feeling.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:36.835
In my senior year, in my
room at the graduate house,

00:02:36.835 --> 00:02:42.310
I remember feeling a bit
overwhelmed myself for what

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:45.860
the future might bring.

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:50.600
Little did I know that
a few years later, I

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:54.080
will be deeply involved
in politics, elected

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:56.720
to the House of
Representatives only

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:01.700
to have Congress shut down
as violence against my party

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:03.350
broke out.

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:08.450
A year later, I returned
to Boston with my new wife

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:12.080
to obtain a graduate
degree at MIT.

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:15.050
Soon after, my first
child, Carolina,

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:19.850
was born here at
Massachusetts General Hospital

00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:21.770
at the same time my
friend and adjunct

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:28.146
professor, Paul Samuelson,
and his wife had triplets.

00:03:28.146 --> 00:03:33.820
I knew I had to return to
Colombia in a time of crisis.

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:37.820
But little did I know what
extraordinary events would

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:40.810
shape my life in public service.

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:44.930
I have learned that
our lives and careers

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:51.080
are shaped by outside forces
often beyond our control.

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:55.760
Throughout our lives, we are
faced with important choices.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:59.480
In the end, I
realize how important

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:04.010
it is to establish a set
of values and beliefs

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:07.550
to guide these choices.

00:04:07.550 --> 00:04:12.650
I also realize that a strong
foundation in the humanities,

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:16.459
economics, and
technology was the key,

00:04:16.459 --> 00:04:19.790
for it's through these
basic disciplines

00:04:19.790 --> 00:04:22.190
that we manage change.

00:04:22.190 --> 00:04:26.390
They are truly at the
core of social progress.

00:04:26.390 --> 00:04:30.170
Now these enormously
powerful tools

00:04:30.170 --> 00:04:34.280
of change rest in your
hands, and the fate

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:38.480
of future generations
depends on your ability

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:43.490
to put to a good use all
that you have learned.

00:04:43.490 --> 00:04:46.970
Your families, your
professors, and I

00:04:46.970 --> 00:04:52.541
are all confident that each
of you can meet the challenge.

00:04:52.541 --> 00:04:58.990
This pace of change under
rigors of academic life

00:04:58.990 --> 00:05:03.940
have, at times, I am sure,
left you gasping for breath,

00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:07.270
wondering if the world
of learning around you

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:10.040
will ever slow down.

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:14.360
Today would you kindly
stop and take a breath?

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:18.400
Look back on the years
you have spent at MIT.

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:22.416
Look beyond the long
hours and hard work.

00:05:22.416 --> 00:05:26.990
What you have achieved
today is a true compliment

00:05:26.990 --> 00:05:32.030
to all of you, and you should
stop and savor the moment.

00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:36.295
Commit to memory the
time you have spent here

00:05:36.295 --> 00:05:38.690
and what you have done.

00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:40.970
In the future,
you will look back

00:05:40.970 --> 00:05:45.680
on these years as a time
of great excitement,

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:49.530
of great challenges,
and of great ideas.

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:55.220
It was a time when many
of life's mundane details

00:05:55.220 --> 00:06:00.740
could be set aside, so you could
focus on ideas, on learning,

00:06:00.740 --> 00:06:05.150
on experiencing the
thrill of academic life.

00:06:05.150 --> 00:06:09.380
You will look back at
these years as a time

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:13.070
when life was lived to
its fullest, when bonds

00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:15.380
of friendship were built--

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:19.055
bonds that will last
your entire lives.

00:06:19.055 --> 00:06:24.230
Never forget that what
you have learned here.

00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:26.720
It will serve you well
for years to come.

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:31.076
Indeed, it has
served me very well.

00:06:31.076 --> 00:06:36.870
In many ways, this is also
a proud day in my life.

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:41.160
Today I also find myself
poised at the edge

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:43.660
of a new time in my life.

00:06:43.660 --> 00:06:49.700
This speech to the class
of 1990 at my alma mater

00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:53.970
will be one of the last major
speeches of my presidency.

00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:57.270
Soon I will depart
elected office

00:06:57.270 --> 00:07:01.170
and seek new
challenges in my life.

00:07:01.170 --> 00:07:05.750
Some have asked how I
manage the job of president

00:07:05.750 --> 00:07:07.710
at such difficult times.

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:13.230
I tell them that it is nothing
compared to taking the oral PhD

00:07:13.230 --> 00:07:14.820
examination at MIT.

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:16.700
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:18.580
[APPLAUSE]

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:26.940
In many ways, my situation
is much like yours.

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:30.975
Together we enter a
new time in our lives,

00:07:30.975 --> 00:07:36.930
a time with new opportunities
and new responsibilities.

00:07:36.930 --> 00:07:40.950
We must enter this
new time bravely,

00:07:40.950 --> 00:07:43.530
holding dearly to
the values that we

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:46.660
have been taught
to cherish at home,

00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:49.580
at school, and here at MIT.

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:54.190
No doubt-- all of
you expect me to talk

00:07:54.190 --> 00:07:57.580
about an issue that
has stirred the hearts

00:07:57.580 --> 00:08:03.030
and minds and emotions of
millions around the world.

00:08:03.030 --> 00:08:08.330
It is an issue I have
come to represent,

00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:13.260
one which has even led to some
controversy in this campus.

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:18.010
I am sure you expected me
to use this time with you

00:08:18.010 --> 00:08:22.660
to discuss the scourge
of illegal narcotics.

00:08:22.660 --> 00:08:28.630
But that is not what I have
come here to speak about today.

00:08:28.630 --> 00:08:33.250
I hope my views on this
issue are well known by now.

00:08:33.250 --> 00:08:36.640
Every day you read
about our struggle

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:40.390
against narco-terrorism and
drug trafficking in Colombia.

00:08:40.390 --> 00:08:43.179
This struggle will
continue when I

00:08:43.179 --> 00:08:48.700
leave office, for good men
and women everywhere will not

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:53.320
tolerate misery and violence
bred by those who push

00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:55.982
and those who consume
illegal drugs.

00:08:55.982 --> 00:08:57.950
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:03.370 --> 00:09:04.690
No.

00:09:04.690 --> 00:09:10.090
Today I want to explore with you
a wider vision and the events

00:09:10.090 --> 00:09:15.265
that will shape a new global
order in the 21st century.

00:09:15.265 --> 00:09:20.410
For all days, a day which has
such personal [? importance ?]

00:09:20.410 --> 00:09:26.290
to me and to each of you, this
is the day to look forward,

00:09:26.290 --> 00:09:29.200
not to look back.

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:32.720
Like more of you
here, in Colombia,

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:38.650
we watched on television
the wall come down in Berlin

00:09:38.650 --> 00:09:41.550
with rapt attention.

00:09:41.550 --> 00:09:46.340
From people of power in
the Philippines to Václav

00:09:46.340 --> 00:09:51.570
Havel's Velvet Revolution
to the no vote in Chile,

00:09:51.570 --> 00:09:56.760
democracy is on the rise around
the globe as totalitarian

00:09:56.760 --> 00:10:02.790
governments are tossed off by
people restless to experience

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:04.290
a world of freedom.

00:10:04.290 --> 00:10:07.590
For all these devoted
to the cause of freedom,

00:10:07.590 --> 00:10:12.063
we share in the
celebration of democracy.

00:10:12.063 --> 00:10:13.987
[APPLAUSE]

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:24.440
Democracy is not a distant
notion at all in Colombia.

00:10:24.440 --> 00:10:30.130
Indeed, our position in Latin
America is somewhat unique.

00:10:30.130 --> 00:10:34.510
Our constitution is
nearly as old as yours,

00:10:34.510 --> 00:10:37.900
and our democratic
institutions have long

00:10:37.900 --> 00:10:42.320
been a model to our neighbors.

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:47.050
This is the true significance
of your current struggle,

00:10:47.050 --> 00:10:49.900
for the greatest
threat to our democracy

00:10:49.900 --> 00:10:54.460
is narco-terrorism and the
insatiable worldwide demand

00:10:54.460 --> 00:10:57.580
for drug which fuels it.

00:10:57.580 --> 00:11:03.340
In the past, we usually
saw extremist ideologies

00:11:03.340 --> 00:11:07.060
as the most serious
threat to democracy.

00:11:07.060 --> 00:11:12.070
But now drugs and organized
crime are even more dangerous--

00:11:12.070 --> 00:11:17.640
not only to our democracy,
but to yours as well.

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:23.430
This is an auspicious moment
to reflect on our changing

00:11:23.430 --> 00:11:27.540
world and a new global order.

00:11:27.540 --> 00:11:32.860
Look at what has happened
since I graduated from MIT.

00:11:32.860 --> 00:11:39.010
In 1943, a terrible war was
raging in Europe and in Asia,

00:11:39.010 --> 00:11:45.280
taking lives and devastating
the countryside at every turn.

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:49.960
No one can ever forget
the tragedy of this war--

00:11:49.960 --> 00:11:55.900
the 45 million killed; the Nazi
death camps; the destruction

00:11:55.900 --> 00:11:58.120
of towns, cities--

00:11:58.120 --> 00:12:01.610
of lives and hopes of
generations to come--

00:12:01.610 --> 00:12:07.330
the use of atomic weapons
at Hiroshima and Nagasaki.

00:12:07.330 --> 00:12:12.680
Seven years later,
when I returned to MIT

00:12:12.680 --> 00:12:17.000
for postgraduate
studies, Europe was still

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:21.970
rebuilding the ravages of
this war, of the Cold War--

00:12:21.970 --> 00:12:26.200
appeared to be a reality
that will never leave.

00:12:26.200 --> 00:12:29.350
Soviets and Americans
stood poised,

00:12:29.350 --> 00:12:32.860
ready to strike each other
at a moment's notice,

00:12:32.860 --> 00:12:37.420
threatening the existence
of the human race.

00:12:37.420 --> 00:12:41.680
Around the world,
democracy was in retreat,

00:12:41.680 --> 00:12:44.710
and totalitarian
regimes flourished

00:12:44.710 --> 00:12:47.470
on nearly every continent.

00:12:47.470 --> 00:12:52.020
The result of totalitarian
regimes in Latin America

00:12:52.020 --> 00:12:54.850
was the same as everywhere--

00:12:54.850 --> 00:12:58.180
the crushing of the
will of the people,

00:12:58.180 --> 00:13:01.360
the violation of
fundamental human rights,

00:13:01.360 --> 00:13:04.180
the demise of democracy.

00:13:04.180 --> 00:13:08.240
[INAUDIBLE] in this Cold
War, the developed world

00:13:08.240 --> 00:13:12.460
chose to look the other way
as millions in Latin America

00:13:12.460 --> 00:13:15.270
suffer under oppression.

00:13:15.270 --> 00:13:18.700
US foreign policy
remained fixated

00:13:18.700 --> 00:13:20.890
on East-West competition.

00:13:20.890 --> 00:13:25.660
A North-South view
rarely came into focus.

00:13:25.660 --> 00:13:30.820
And yet, in the last
decade, democratic change

00:13:30.820 --> 00:13:33.250
has swept our continent.

00:13:33.250 --> 00:13:37.840
10 years ago, most
countries of Latin America

00:13:37.840 --> 00:13:41.950
suffered under the
weight of dictatorships.

00:13:41.950 --> 00:13:43.625
Just look at the change.

00:13:43.625 --> 00:13:50.520
Peru returned to democracy
in 1980, Bolivia in 1982,

00:13:50.520 --> 00:13:55.660
Argentina in 1983,
Brazil in 1985--

00:13:55.660 --> 00:14:00.280
Chile, Nicaragua, and Panama
in the last year alone.

00:14:00.280 --> 00:14:05.140
Almost a whole continent
has moved from military rule

00:14:05.140 --> 00:14:09.700
to legitimately elected leaders.

00:14:09.700 --> 00:14:13.390
But still, as evidence of
North American [INAUDIBLE]

00:14:13.390 --> 00:14:17.620
to the Cold War ideology,
these dramatic changes

00:14:17.620 --> 00:14:20.650
so close to your
borders received

00:14:20.650 --> 00:14:25.070
relatively little attention.

00:14:25.070 --> 00:14:32.410
Now it's time to open
our eyes to a new world.

00:14:32.410 --> 00:14:38.210
After years of superpower
conflict, each one of you--

00:14:38.210 --> 00:14:43.780
what each one is vying for
gains in the high-stakes game

00:14:43.780 --> 00:14:46.390
for global military advantage.

00:14:46.390 --> 00:14:51.900
We are now able to see beyond
the East-West confrontation.

00:14:51.900 --> 00:14:58.720
Today's global order no
longer rests on the foundation

00:14:58.720 --> 00:15:01.090
of security concerns.

00:15:01.090 --> 00:15:06.820
Military might is no longer what
determines a country's place

00:15:06.820 --> 00:15:09.216
in the international system.

00:15:09.216 --> 00:15:15.540
We are now able to look beyond
the myopia of the Cold War,

00:15:15.540 --> 00:15:18.540
beyond our previously
clouded horizon,

00:15:18.540 --> 00:15:23.440
to where a new world is
waiting; a world where

00:15:23.440 --> 00:15:26.620
economic growth and
technological innovation

00:15:26.620 --> 00:15:31.880
will be of critical importance,
not sheer military strength;

00:15:31.880 --> 00:15:37.630
a world where entrepreneurs and
innovators will lead the way,

00:15:37.630 --> 00:15:41.496
not the generals of yesterday.

00:15:41.496 --> 00:15:48.760
Witness Japan, a country that
spends little on its military

00:15:48.760 --> 00:15:53.700
but that has suddenly leaped
to the front ranks of world

00:15:53.700 --> 00:15:55.110
powers.

00:15:55.110 --> 00:15:58.140
It is a country that
gives more economic aid

00:15:58.140 --> 00:16:01.930
to the third world
than any other nation,

00:16:01.930 --> 00:16:06.960
including historically
generous United States.

00:16:06.960 --> 00:16:10.590
The small military
establishment of Japan

00:16:10.590 --> 00:16:14.010
presents no serious
threat to the vast armies

00:16:14.010 --> 00:16:16.740
of the US or Soviet Union.

00:16:16.740 --> 00:16:20.040
But through its
economic might, it

00:16:20.040 --> 00:16:23.700
wields through global
influence today.

00:16:23.700 --> 00:16:29.790
Which country, I ask, do others
most want to emulate today--

00:16:29.790 --> 00:16:34.080
Japan or the Soviet Union?

00:16:34.080 --> 00:16:38.590
The economic growth
through Asia and in Europe

00:16:38.590 --> 00:16:40.936
has been extraordinary.

00:16:40.936 --> 00:16:45.670
Now we must work to expand
the economic success

00:16:45.670 --> 00:16:49.420
of these dynamic
regions to other areas.

00:16:49.420 --> 00:16:53.890
This is particularly true for
the fragile new democracies

00:16:53.890 --> 00:16:55.150
of the world.

00:16:55.150 --> 00:17:00.250
For these countries to
solidify their political gains,

00:17:00.250 --> 00:17:03.790
they will need economic success.

00:17:03.790 --> 00:17:08.109
From Poland to Argentina
to the Philippines,

00:17:08.109 --> 00:17:11.920
newly free people
expect economic growth

00:17:11.920 --> 00:17:14.530
and improved living standards.

00:17:14.530 --> 00:17:17.650
And in order to
maintain stability,

00:17:17.650 --> 00:17:21.130
this expectation must be met.

00:17:21.130 --> 00:17:24.640
It is your
responsibility and those

00:17:24.640 --> 00:17:28.750
in other wealthy countries
to ensure the fostering

00:17:28.750 --> 00:17:31.570
of global economic growth.

00:17:31.570 --> 00:17:36.010
In this way, you can help
these fledgling democracies

00:17:36.010 --> 00:17:39.840
as they struggle to
spread their wings.

00:17:39.840 --> 00:17:43.390
This is especially
true in Latin America,

00:17:43.390 --> 00:17:48.040
a region which,
historically, has received

00:17:48.040 --> 00:17:50.500
far too little attention.

00:17:50.500 --> 00:17:55.390
The best way to foster
North-South cooperation

00:17:55.390 --> 00:17:58.090
will not be through
military means

00:17:58.090 --> 00:18:00.760
but rather by
guaranteeing access

00:18:00.760 --> 00:18:04.750
of Latin America's democracies
to economic prosperity

00:18:04.750 --> 00:18:06.880
and change.

00:18:06.880 --> 00:18:13.840
Just as history will
record the 1980s as an era

00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:17.110
when democratic forces
swept the globe.

00:18:17.110 --> 00:18:23.140
It will also be remembered
as a decade of resurgence

00:18:23.140 --> 00:18:26.080
in free market economics.

00:18:26.080 --> 00:18:30.190
This is a revolution of
enormous significance,

00:18:30.190 --> 00:18:33.580
a recognition of
our global economy

00:18:33.580 --> 00:18:37.870
and a confirmation that
open economies with access

00:18:37.870 --> 00:18:41.560
to markets can lead
to social progress.

00:18:41.560 --> 00:18:44.020
It seems that the
long-running match

00:18:44.020 --> 00:18:50.170
between Karl Marx and Adam Smith
is finally coming to an end.

00:18:50.170 --> 00:18:52.130
[APPLAUSE]

00:18:56.540 --> 00:18:59.885
I applaud this development.

00:18:59.885 --> 00:19:06.020
But at the risk of sounding
passé, let me issue a warning.

00:19:06.020 --> 00:19:10.140
In our rush to embrace
free market forces,

00:19:10.140 --> 00:19:15.810
let us not lose sight of the
fundamental role of government.

00:19:15.810 --> 00:19:21.420
It embodies the free and
collective will of its people.

00:19:21.420 --> 00:19:24.510
There is a role for
both market forces

00:19:24.510 --> 00:19:28.470
and the state in
solving social problems.

00:19:28.470 --> 00:19:31.260
Free market policies
should not be

00:19:31.260 --> 00:19:35.760
used as an excuse for the
lack of political will

00:19:35.760 --> 00:19:40.710
where it is a sure injustice or
fight in our drug trafficking

00:19:40.710 --> 00:19:45.480
or providing basic
education and health care.

00:19:45.480 --> 00:19:49.170
Free market economics
is not a magic wand

00:19:49.170 --> 00:19:54.030
which, somehow, will relieve
us of the obligation to care

00:19:54.030 --> 00:19:55.830
for our fellow human beings.

00:19:58.600 --> 00:20:00.600
[APPLAUSE]

00:20:04.100 --> 00:20:08.900
Nowhere is the role
of the state as

00:20:08.900 --> 00:20:12.500
clear as with
another global issue,

00:20:12.500 --> 00:20:16.700
which has been pushed
aside for too long--

00:20:16.700 --> 00:20:20.012
the preservation
of our environment.

00:20:20.012 --> 00:20:25.040
This is a struggle that must be
one of our highest priorities

00:20:25.040 --> 00:20:27.410
in the decade of the '90s.

00:20:27.410 --> 00:20:31.460
I firmly believe that the
industrialized countries have

00:20:31.460 --> 00:20:34.190
an ecological debt to humanity.

00:20:37.648 --> 00:20:39.624
[APPLAUSE]

00:20:42.600 --> 00:20:47.790
In less than two centuries,
industrial development

00:20:47.790 --> 00:20:53.250
has not only destroyed most of
the native forests of Europe

00:20:53.250 --> 00:20:59.130
and North America, but also has
brought pollution, acid rain,

00:20:59.130 --> 00:21:02.670
and destruction to
the ozone layer.

00:21:02.670 --> 00:21:06.480
This is an ecological debt
to future generations, who

00:21:06.480 --> 00:21:09.730
will have to live
with the consequences

00:21:09.730 --> 00:21:14.070
of our thoughtless ways in which
the developed countries have

00:21:14.070 --> 00:21:17.310
achieved their
standard of living.

00:21:17.310 --> 00:21:22.290
The burden of sustaining a
viable planetary environment

00:21:22.290 --> 00:21:27.990
now rests clearly on the
shoulders of the third world,

00:21:27.990 --> 00:21:34.140
for we are the last
frontier of unspoiled lands.

00:21:34.140 --> 00:21:38.550
The only way our countries
can meet this challenge

00:21:38.550 --> 00:21:43.920
is by defeating rural poverty
and economic stagnation.

00:21:43.920 --> 00:21:46.530
The best way for
the United States

00:21:46.530 --> 00:21:48.870
and other
industrialized countries

00:21:48.870 --> 00:21:52.890
to pay their ecological
debt to humanity

00:21:52.890 --> 00:21:55.800
is to be the partners
in this cause.

00:21:55.800 --> 00:21:59.630
My administration
has already set aside

00:21:59.630 --> 00:22:06.380
more than 40 million acres of
rainforest as Indian reserves

00:22:06.380 --> 00:22:13.310
in the Amazon region, an area
larger than the six New England

00:22:13.310 --> 00:22:15.863
states together.

00:22:15.863 --> 00:22:17.827
[APPLAUSE]

00:22:20.282 --> 00:22:26.800
Let us pledge to seek some
development policies which

00:22:26.800 --> 00:22:32.140
recognize the value of our
most precious resources.

00:22:32.140 --> 00:22:36.340
Let us pledge to
retire this debt now

00:22:36.340 --> 00:22:37.690
for future generations.

00:22:40.856 --> 00:22:42.840
[APPLAUSE]

00:22:42.840 --> 00:22:45.600
You are, indeed,
as fortunate as I

00:22:45.600 --> 00:22:49.890
was to be embarking
on new careers

00:22:49.890 --> 00:22:52.390
during a watershed period.

00:22:52.390 --> 00:22:57.720
The record of human history
is marked by these moments

00:22:57.720 --> 00:22:59.940
of fundamental change.

00:22:59.940 --> 00:23:03.900
You will now inherit
a new global order

00:23:03.900 --> 00:23:09.920
with new challenges and
extraordinary opportunities.

00:23:09.920 --> 00:23:16.070
As you watched the joy of your
young German colleagues dancing

00:23:16.070 --> 00:23:20.450
in the streets or the sorrow in
the faces of those brave young

00:23:20.450 --> 00:23:24.650
students at Tiananmen
Square or the courage

00:23:24.650 --> 00:23:28.490
of young Colombian soldiers
fighting for democracy

00:23:28.490 --> 00:23:32.390
against narco-terrorists, it
may have been difficult for you

00:23:32.390 --> 00:23:35.960
here on this beautiful
campus in Boston

00:23:35.960 --> 00:23:40.610
to truly appreciate
such democratic fervor.

00:23:40.610 --> 00:23:45.660
Here in America, where so much
is so often taken for granted,

00:23:45.660 --> 00:23:50.600
the struggle for democracy
may, indeed, seem distant.

00:23:50.600 --> 00:23:54.230
However, you are all
new graduates of one

00:23:54.230 --> 00:23:58.940
of the world's most prestigious
academic institutions.

00:23:58.940 --> 00:24:03.790
That honor brings with it
a special responsibility.

00:24:03.790 --> 00:24:05.840
The new democracies
around the world

00:24:05.840 --> 00:24:09.800
are fragile and need
your help to survive.

00:24:09.800 --> 00:24:14.450
As you look to your
future, I ask one thing.

00:24:14.450 --> 00:24:22.010
Do not let today's flowering
democracies wither on the vine.

00:24:22.010 --> 00:24:23.930
[APPLAUSE]

00:24:27.780 --> 00:24:33.550
Extend your arms to those
who have, for too long,

00:24:33.550 --> 00:24:38.890
lived in oppression, in
fear, and in poverty.

00:24:38.890 --> 00:24:43.150
Like all the newborns,
these infant democracies

00:24:43.150 --> 00:24:48.040
around the world may, at
times, falter and stumble,

00:24:48.040 --> 00:24:50.950
hesitantly trying
their first steps.

00:24:50.950 --> 00:24:54.220
Yet they must prevail.

00:24:54.220 --> 00:24:56.170
This is our first duty.

00:24:56.170 --> 00:25:00.580
All of us, especially you
here in the United States,

00:25:00.580 --> 00:25:05.770
now have an opportunity
to build in peace what

00:25:05.770 --> 00:25:08.500
is often unavailable by force.

00:25:08.500 --> 00:25:11.320
It is our collective
responsibility

00:25:11.320 --> 00:25:17.460
to ensure that this opportunity
does not pass us by.

00:25:17.460 --> 00:25:20.820
You have been well trained.

00:25:20.820 --> 00:25:24.960
I have no doubt that you
have the intellectual power

00:25:24.960 --> 00:25:27.360
to face any challenge.

00:25:27.360 --> 00:25:31.110
I harbor the hopes
that you will also

00:25:31.110 --> 00:25:36.210
have the heart to meet these
challenges with fairness

00:25:36.210 --> 00:25:37.680
and compassion.

00:25:37.680 --> 00:25:39.330
I know you will.

00:25:39.330 --> 00:25:43.410
For this, after all,
is our shared heritage.

00:25:43.410 --> 00:25:50.100
And you, my friends, are now the
guardians of our shared vision.

00:25:50.100 --> 00:25:50.670
Thank you.

00:25:50.670 --> 00:25:51.870
[APPLAUSE]

00:25:56.370 --> 00:25:58.170
Thank you.

00:25:58.170 --> 00:25:59.370
Thank you.

00:25:59.370 --> 00:26:01.220
Thank you.

